# Envisioning young teachers in universities and colleges as “reflective practitioners”: a strategy to address the dilemma of academic work

Abstract: The world is undergoing unprecedent technological, social, and economic changes that are affecting various fields, including education. Concepts once exclusive to industry are now relevant in higher education. For example, the emergence of audit culture in higher education has turned young teachers in universities and colleges into audited individuals and disciplined objects. At present, it is unproductive to blindly hope for fundamental change in the system of academic management. Thus, young teachers in universities and colleges must reconsider the nature of academic work, break through the limitations of traditional epistemology, and become reflective practitioners. This transformation is crucial for improving the academic environment and clarifying the roles of contemporary teachers. In response to academic dilemmas such as the lack of harmony between teaching and research, the pervasive anxiety in academic life, and implicit manipulative practices in academic management, young teachers should reflect on academic work through strategic, evaluative, moral, and identity lenses by asking questions like “How can it be done?”, “What is accomplished?”, “Why?”, and “Who should I be?”. Such a change would promote educational reform, help re-establish the meaning and ideal of academic work, and truly promote the teaching and learning of young teachers in universities and colleges.

Key words: Academic work; Reflective practitioners; Change paths; Problem framing

# Proposition of the Research Question

As higher education enters the stage of massification, universities at all levels have embraced an audit culture influenced by a new culture of academic managerialism. In an era where knowledge is rapidly evaluated based on its “validity” and “marketability,” university teachers can only secure their positions in the academic community by producing more “valid” knowledge. Theses, research projects, and professional titles have become the three essential tools for young teachers to survive in this environment of academic involution. This academic environment is characterized by intense competition and an overemphasis on quantitative measures of success, often at the expense of genuine innovation and intellectual contributions. According to literature recorded in the Web of Science database, “China has ranked second in the world in the number of SCI papers published for 10 consecutive years, and by May 2021, China has overtaken the United States to occupy the top spot.” However, the actual innovation rate is far less impressive.

Interestingly, despite the academic world being aware of the drawbacks of the existing system for academic management and evaluation, the pace of reform has been extremely slow. The academic environment faced by young teachers has not improved; rather, it has become more challenging. To understand the current academic situation and the claims and actions of young teachers, it is necessary to change perspectives and analytical frameworks and develop a new strategy for managing academic work. Based on these considerations, this paper proposes that teachers in universities and colleges need to reconsider the nature of academic work and become reflective practitioners. Reflective practitioners critically analyse their own teaching and research practices to continuously improve and adapt to changing academic environments. Only in this way can researchers see the benefits of modifications to university management and rediscover the meaning and value of academic work.

# The Nature and Predicament of Academic Work

In the 1990s, the American scholar Boyer (1990) redefined the concept of academic work in the research report “Academic Reflection,” submitted to the Carnegie Association for the Advancement of Teaching. He proposed that teachers in universities and colleges should undertake four types of scholarship: “the scholarship of discovery; the scholarship of integration; the scholarship of application; and the scholarship of teaching” (p. 16). This definition greatly expanded the common understanding of the nature of academic work and aroused extensive debate among scholars. Johnston (1998) proposed that in the future development of universities, it is necessary to both maintain and expand the definition of “academic” proposed by Boyer” (pp. 253-272). Similarly, Diamond (2002) suggested that the definition of academic work in the 21st century should be broader (pp. 73-80). Kalleberg (2000) regarded academic work as a series of activities that affect universities, which can be divided into research, education and teaching, external service, public lectures, and administration(pp.219-257). Chinese scholars have paid considerable attention to the academic work of university teachers in recent years. Empirical studies have shown that the academic responsibilities of university and college teachers primarily revolve around four key areas: teaching, research, public service, and administration (Cornford, 2002, p.219). Teaching involves imparting knowledge and guiding student development, while research focuses on advancing innovation and contributing to the academic field. Public service includes providing expertise and support to the community, and administrative duties involve participating in the day-to-day management and organization of their departments or institutions. Together, these elements form the core of academic work for educators in higher education. As academic work shifts from professionalization to institutionalization, “slow time”—unstructured time for deep thinking and reflection—has become out of reach. University and college teachers, as professionals, frequently find themselves entangled in a myriad of tasks that are often disjointed and seemingly minor. Their daily responsibilities tend to be fragmented, involving numerous administrative duties, committee work, teaching, and research-related activities, which can make it difficult to maintain focus on long-term, meaningful academic pursuits (Berg, 2016, p.168). The expansion of the scope of academic work has become a common fact. The “academic work” mentioned in this article is also a “pan-academic” concept that reflects the actual state of the academic system; that is, it includes many practices within education and teaching, research, public service, administrative management, etc. With the new prevalence of audit culture in academic management, today’s contemporary young scholars face more anxiety and pressure compared with the older generation of scholars and are more likely to become victims of academic competition. In this study, through surveys and interviews with 22 young teachers in universities and colleges, particularly those who had just received their doctoral degrees, we found that young teachers face many difficulties in their academic work, including “the impracticality of teaching and research promoting and enhancing each other,” “pan-anxiety in academic life,” and “implicit manipulative practices (PUA) in academic management.”

## The Impracticality of Teaching and Research Promoting and Enhancing Each Other

Obtaining a harmonious relationship between teaching and research has always been a complex and challenging issue in academia. The focus of the discourse on this topic has changed from “Which side is more important?” to “How can the two better promote each other?” The development of post work in colleges and universities and the scientific research-oriented evaluation system that overemphasizes timeliness make the harmonious integration of research and teaching and seem unrealistic. Many young teachers have immediately taken on a lot of teaching work after graduation without any adaptation or transition period. Long-term, high-intensity teaching will inevitably result in pressure and fatigue among young teachers. Due to their minimal teaching experience, young teachers at this stage do not focus on how scientific research can help their teaching or on effectively combining teaching and research; rather, they devote most of their time and energy to lesson preparation to allow them to complete the assigned amount of teaching work. Higher education, by its very nature, is centered on the pursuit and advancement of specialized and complex knowledge. Therefore, teaching at the university level should not simply be about delivering information; it must integrate both educational practice and academic inquiry (Boyer, 1990). This means that university educators should blend teaching with cutting-edge research, fostering an environment where students are not only learning established knowledge but are also engaged in the exploration of new ideas and discoveries. Teaching without incorporating scientific research, even when using advanced technology and effective teaching methods, will not provide high-quality education or encourage deep thinking. 

However, for new Ph.D. graduates, their research careers have just begun, and they have not yet accumulated a substantial body of research. Simultaneously, they struggle to cope with numerous evaluations (e.g., professional title evaluations, position assessments, and promotions). Under these circumstances, activities like teaching supervision and teaching skills training, which are intended to educate and improve professionalism, may become additional burdens for young teachers. Consequently, it becomes difficult to achieve high-quality teaching, and time for research is significantly reduced. Zheng Qiang,[[wn1\]](#_msocom_1) the former Secretary of the Party Committee of Taiyuan University of Technology, said the following in a public interview: “*When a newly graduated doctor is engaged in an university, if he does not catch time to make achievements in academics and carry out book education too early, then he will not only be unable to show competent performance in class, but also delay his golden period of academic exploration*. *Fresh-graded doctors need spend at least 3 to 5 years, mainly engaged in scientific research. When they reach the age of 45 to 50, with a certain academic experience and even academic achievements, they could start to turn their honors and happiness they have received into education**”* (The Propaganda Department of the Party Committee, 2020).Thus, the mutual promotion of teaching and research in academic work depends not only on the strength of young scholars, but quite realistic constraints.

## Pan-anxiety in Academic Life

In contemporary society, anxiety has gradually become generalized and is now the norm. We seem to have entered an era of “national anxiety.” Bowman believes that anxiety has become a basic condition of modern daily life, with more and more people immersed in an endless fear of risk on one hand and a struggle to establish a firm sense of self on the other. The academic world is increasingly embracing a culture driven by the logic of competitive individualism (Müller, 2019, p.162). In this environment, individual scholars are often pressured to compete against one another for limited resources, such as research funding, tenure positions, and publication opportunities. This culture emphasizes personal achievement, productivity metrics, and rankings, often at the expense of collaboration and collective progress. As a result, teachers in universities and colleges realize their self-worth only by constant competition with others, and they are continuously faced with uncertainties brought about by the ever-changing demands of modern life.

From a microscopic point of view, the accelerated pace of academic life and the fragmentation of teachers’ autonomy and disposable time have led to the phenomenon of pan-anxiety. The working hours of teachers in universities and colleges are much higher than the legal working hours. Wankat (2002) reported that “for most teachers, at least 55 hours of work per week is required to complete the task well” (p. 18). Although teachers in universities and colleges have enviable winter and summer vacations each year, “the weekly working hours of teachers in winter and summer vacations are still as long as 32.9 hours ”(Liu, 2015,p.56). [[wn2\]](#_msocom_2) 

Although the working hours in academia are flexible, this professional advantage is also an important source of trouble and stress; it blurs the boundary between work and life and means that work is on the minds of scholars every moment. For teachers in universities and colleges, they need compete for almost all resources from securing and retaining employment, job evaluation and promotion, research funding and awards, academic titles, reputation acquisition, journal publications, and research breakthroughs. To win the competition and obtain more resources, teachers have to speed up like hamsters placed on an exercise wheel.

In this context, the acceleration of the individual leads to the acceleration of the collective, which in turn raises the threshold of excellence. This situation has created a distorted reality in which scholars who do not advance or only make small advances functionally regress. All scholars must speed up to keep pace with others and maintain a competitive advantage. Competition thus accelerates the pace of academic life. Compared with teachers who have more seniority, pan-anxiety is more pronounced among young teachers in universities and colleges and has a greater impact on their academic careers.

## Implicit PUA in Academic Management

Workplace PUA, a concept that has gained popularity in recent years, describes how superiors in the workplace influence, manage, and manipulate the mindset and behavior of their subordinates (Wang, 2022, p.357). The most common example is when superiors demean and suppress the personality and dignity of their workers through words or actions, criticize their subordinates for lack of ambition, and fail to provide adequate support while maintaining high performance expectations and standards. One distinctive feature of workplace PUA is an emphasis on subordinates’ unconditional obedience to leaders. Leaders require work that is beyond the scope of the job and requires employees to be on call to complete work tasks during off hours. Although the term PUA has recently been applied to describe academic workplaces, research on the PUA phenomenon in academic management is essentially nonexistent. This situation is a natural result of the new managerialism and performance-oriented corporate culture in the academic setting.

Due to the particularity of academic power, PUA in academic management is often not as explicit as in other fields, and it is closely related to the type, level, and culture of the institution. In a research university with a strong academic reputation, the implementation of corporate culture and administrative power will be more difficult and vice versa. Although academic managers cannot implement PUA on a broad scale, they can manage and control academic staff by implementing strict supervision and frequent evaluation, similar to the subtle power techniques described by Foucault[[wn3\]](#_msocom_3) (Foucault, 2023, p. 291).

This “implicit PUA” in academic management reflects the lack of appreciation for academic freedom, human dignity, and interpersonal relationships among academic administrators. Administrators exert their power by strengthening assessment protocols, requiring scientific research output, and issuing “tasks,” all of which distort the nature of academic work. Under this management structure, authentic and credible social relationships are replaced by a rigid evaluation-based framework. These administrators regard academic work, especially the academic activities of young scholars, as a process of maintaining a specific order through the creation of various identities, honors, and titles along with a competitive institutional environment that promotes “research performance.” This approach embeds academic work within a social order that is completely controlled by administrative power. As a result, scholars engaged in academic work, especially young teachers in universities and colleges, cannot help thinking: What is the importance of academic work?

# Reflective Practice: A Possibility for Action

In view of the findings above, whether due to the lack of harmony between research and teaching, the pan-anxiety of academic life, or the implicit PUA in academic management, young teachers in universities and colleges find themselves in the following situation: What we do (even if we voluntarily do it) is not the state of what we really want to do. As a result, young teachers encounter an academic environment full of trust, tolerance, and support but are confused about the meaning of academic work and life. Given the challenging and passive work environment faced by many young teachers, a significant number of them have developed a pessimistic outlook (Bullough, 2011, p.127). They are keenly aware of the flaws in the broader academic management system and its limitations. Consequently, many have put forward suggestions for reform, offering ideas to improve the academic community by making it more supportive and conducive to both professional growth and meaningful academic contributions. However, the following question remains: Does the ideal academic community still exist? If it does not exist, how can we have productive discussions within the community, and how can we reproduce the community that has been lost? Under these circumstances, if young scholars can reflect on the practice of academic work (i.e., “reflective practice”), at least they can know what to prepare for. Reflective practice involves critically analyzing one’s own teaching and research practices to continuously improve and adapt to changing academic environments. This approach can help young teachers navigate the complexities of academic life and find greater meaning and satisfaction in their work.

Reflective practice is a theory of professional practice put forward by Professor Donald Schon of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. Deeply influenced by Dewey’s philosophy of empirical naturalism, Schon(2018) believes that “scientific and technological rationality is essentially a kind of positivist epistemology characterized by prioritizing scientific theory and technology in practice”(p.31). Meanwhile, practice is regarded only as a form of descriptive knowledge of the world or an analytical model of logic and mathematics. Practical knowledge is constructed as knowledge of the “method-goal” relationship. That is, if the goal is determined, the question of how people will act is reduced to “What is the most effective means to achieve the goal?”. Practical knowledge itself is also dyed with a strong instrumental color. Positivist epistemology bases the legitimacy of professional practice on the “specialization, clear boundaries, scientism, and standardization” of scientific and technological knowledge (Schön, 2018, p.24). Thus, professional practice vacillates between scientism and instrumentalism, which affects both the activities of professional practice and the institutional culture of institutes of higher education. In Schon’s (2018) view, the real world confronting professional practice is “constructed by the sum of confusing, distressing, and undetermined problem situations”(p.36). Therefore, actual professional practice is not a simple process of technical problem-solving; rather, it is a process of solving problems through understanding, reflections, and interaction in complex situations followed by achieving the goal. In reflective practical epistemology, professional workers reflect their own patterns of action in their daily professional activities: “They reflect on action, and also reflect in action.”

What is reflection? Donald Schon (2018) believes that reflection is not just thinking but a dialogue (self and self; self and others) that includes the expression of thought, emotion, and behavior. Reflection includes “reflecting in action, knowing in action and reflecting on action”(Schon, 2018, pp.46-47) .“Reflecting in action” is the process of reshaping the ongoing action as the situation changes; in this process, the practitioner affects the subsequent situation and is the cause and effect of “accidents.” “Reflecting in action” also requires practitioners to diagnose problems, find knowledge in databases, and construct contexts(Schon, 2018). “Knowing in action” refers to the indescribable type of “knowing how” manifested in intellectual action(Schon, 2018). In this concept, knowledge happens in action. Knowledge implies the dynamic nature of knowledge in action and, when describing it, transforms it into “knowing in action.” As Schon (2018)points out, “reflecting on action” is a reflection after action, usually with the help of words and symbols, that involves some form of experimentation(pp.52-53). In this kind of experiment, the practitioner attempts to establish and solve the problem. In real practice, the problem is not presented to the practitioner in a clear way; rather, it needs to be constructed in chaotic, complex, and uncertain situations. When the practitioner sets the problem, they select the “thing” that must be dealt with, which defines the scope of the problem and provides direction for the practitioner to determine what needs to be changed about the “thing.”

As an alternative to traditional epistemology (shown in Table 1), what benefits does the concept of reflective practice bring to the academic work of young teachers in universities and colleges? As professional workers, so long as teachers operate within an academic bureaucracy, they are naturally embedded in the organizational knowledge structure and a network of organizational systems related to authority, control, information, and rewards. These structures and systems are closely linked to the dominant technical profession, which emphasizes specialized knowledge and standardized procedures. However, as Schon (2018) puts it, this knowledge is limited to specific techniques and procedures and can “intensify the pre-existing conflict between bureaucracy and professional identity”(p.263). This raises the following question: “How can professional workers identify themselves as autonomous practitioners under the bureaucratic control of a highly specialized and managed workforce?”(Schon ,2018, p.263). How can contemporary young teachers in universities and colleges place themselves at the center of academic work, instead of being reduced to “human resources” developed and utilized by universities under the control of performance management and technological rationality? Here, technological rationality refers to the logic and methods derived from the application of scientific and technological principles to solve problems, often emphasizing efficiency and control.

In view of the concept of reflective practice, when contemporary young teachers in universities and colleges can “reflect in academic work,” “know in academic work,” and “reflect on their academic work,” they can become “reflective practitioners” who actively construct their own professional practice. When young teachers in universities and colleges attempt to apply reflective practices in their own academic work, they may be navigating and negotiating with rigid school management systems in their own way. This approach allows young teachers to adapt to and potentially influence the administrative structures they work within, fostering a more dynamic and responsive academic environment.

As a result, the conflicts and contradictions within the academic system (e.g., centralized curriculum plans, excessive paperwork, and mandatory bureaucratic duties) have the opportunity to surface. When young teachers engage in constructive public inquiry, they challenge the theoretical knowledge underlying the system to avoid becoming mere “skilled workers.” If a young teacher becomes a “reflective practitioner,” they will reflect on their academic work, beliefs about their professional actions, and performance evaluations under the existing academic system. When asking these questions, the teacher essentially reflects on the knowledge structure of the organization. Therefore, when academic workers critically reflect on their practical activities, they also critically reflect on those ideas and values that have been ignored by the organization. “What is reflected in action is not just implicit assumptions and techniques, but also values and intentions framed by organizational structures” (Schön, 2018, p.264). As a result, academic organizations must often reserve space to focus on conflicting values and intentions, creating opportunities for transformative change. Changing the academic system so that it is no longer just a “utopia in words” then becomes a possibility.

