# IV. An Attempt to Solve the Academic Dilemma by Creating Reflective Practitioners

If young teachers in universities and colleges want to improve the academic system from the bottom up, they need to become reflective practitioners. In view of the rich connotation of reflection, young teachers in universities and colleges need to “reflect in academic work,” “know in academic work,” and “reflect on academic work.” This series of practical activities can be regarded as a kind of “action full of thought” and “thought full of action” (Evans, 2007,pp.553-559).This approach emphasizes both criticism of existing practice and exploring the underlying purposes of these practices, calling for “a greater awareness of what is possible ”(Räsänen, 2009,pp.185-195). Gibbs and Costley (2007) drew on Aristotle’s concept of practical wisdom to advocate for the educational concept of “ethics of care” and regarded practice as a specific political or moral course of action. In the context of existing academic institutions, scholars can establish a reflective practice that is “capable of finding ways to engage in this selected set of practices with personal meaning and purpose,” where “themes, principles, ethics, and tactics are unified and coherent.” If an academic worker conducts reflective practice in their work, they will understand who they are along with their ethical motivations, professional goals, and action strategies. As Räsänen(2009) put it, despite “internal contradictions, it is alive, meaningful, possible, to be a hope, a way of expressing what might be, and a way of expressing potential”(p.185).

## How can it be done?

“How can it be done?” is a strategic question (de Certeau, 1984)  that relates to how teachers in universities and colleges are “reflected in academic work.” As professional workers, teachers in universities and colleges take practical action in response to the various demands of academic work. When answering this question, each academic worker will gradually develop their own identity and role. Who the worker becomes ultimately depends on the problems they face, the problems they select, and the strategies they choose for action. 

## What is accomplished?

“What is accomplished” is an evaluative question that reflects how teachers in universities and colleges need to reflect in academic work and know in academic work. When a teacher turns their attention to action, they must consider what is accomplished and achieved. The current academic system is constantly changing, and there are many options for what to accomplish and achieve; there are also many competing interests that must be considered. When a teacher “reflects in academic work,” the goals they reflect upon may vary with the situation and their “practical knowledge.” By reflecting, the teacher can expose and critique the tacit knowledge of repetitive experience in a specialized practice and generate new understandings of the uncertainty or uniqueness of the situation.

## Are the methods and goals worthwhile and reasonable?

“Are the methods and goals worthwhile and reasonable?” is a moral question (Schön, 2018) that is meant to address the question of “Why?”. This question indicates that teachers in universities and colleges should not only “reflect in academic work” and “know in academic work,” but also “reflect on academic work.” This differs from the practice that takes scientific and technological rationality as the logic of action under the control of traditional practical epistemology, which pursues the most effective method to achieve goals. In reality, teachers in universities and colleges are faced with many conflicting paradigms in their academic work. Although they must rely on the scientific and technological rational model, these conflicts can only be resolved by another method of inquiry. This method requires self-judgment from the standpoint of morality and inquiries about the rationality of the method and the goal itself.

## Who should I be?

“Who should I be?” is a question of personal identity (Dreier, 1999). Young teachers in universities and colleges gradually form their own understanding by “reflecting in academic work,” “knowing in academic work,” and “reflecting in academic work,” and these actions will eventually shape the direction of the teacher. New teachers in universities and colleges are in the critical period of deciding what their identity is. In the face of complex academic work, these teachers examine their own principles and values, consider the conflicting values of their organization, and conduct their own reflective practice, ultimately discovering and defining themselves.

The order of the above four basic questions is important, with the strategic question (how) taking precedence over the evaluative (what is accomplished) and moral (why) questions. During actual work, young teachers in universities and colleges face urgent tasks and severe time pressures, which can intuitively reveal a scholar’s competence, survival skills, and job performance and highlight their focus on “performance metrics.” For example, a young teacher might be teaching a certain course within a given time while conducting scientific research and writing research reports. In this scenario, the strategic question of “How can it be done?” is the first priority; only after accumulating experience can a teacher address the evaluative question (“What is accomplished and achieved?”) and evaluate their work achievements and contributions. Questioning and reflecting on the value and purpose of current educational practice from a moral standpoint is a deeper and more difficult “reflective practice ”(Åkerlind, 2003, pp.375-390). If the discussion turns directly to evaluative questions, it could re-ignite common debates about the current academic governance system. In contrast, if moral issues are considered first, the discussion will take the form of an exchange of general opinions or even devolve into preaching.

This framework of questions allows space for different strategies of action, principles, ethics, and academic work itself. This problem framework is not intended to provide a set of standard answers to problems; rather, it aims to encourage a reflective practice. When young teachers in universities and colleges begin to frame their own problems, they will also begin to perceive practical realities from different perspectives. This process allows teachers to determine the values and norms that are important and unimportant to them. This framework can also help teachers perceive the implicit cognitive framework of individuals. Once teachers in universities and colleges perceive that individuals are actively constructing academic practice and begin to perceive other difficulties in academic work, they can seek answers within this framework, and different frameworks can even be developed and used. This means that teachers shape their academic activities based on their reflections and insights. This process of framing problems can help young teachers appreciate the possibilities of their own creation.

In consideration of three phenomena emerging from field research—the impracticality of teaching and research promoting and enhancing each other, the pan-anxiety of academic life, and the implicit PUA in academic management—this paper attempts to guide young teachers in universities and colleges toward the evaluative and moral goals of academic work. By following the framework formed by the four questions discussed above, this paper provides a possible path for contemporary young teachers to thrive in universities and colleges. Table 2 shows examples of the reflective practice implemented by three young teachers in universities and colleges. The survey results suggest that young teachers in universities and colleges do find ways to engage in a range of academic practices. However, reflecting on the strategic question of “How?”, not all strategies are contextual or positive. In the face of implicit PUA in academic management, some young teachers decisively choose to “avoid and not listen” and act according to their own logic. When faced with the pan-anxiety of academic life, some young teachers in universities and colleges reduce their overwhelming workload by actively negotiating and communicating with their superiors. Moreover, the strategic, evaluative, moral, and identity considerations are not always in alignment. For example, in the face of the impracticality of teaching and research promoting and enhancing each other, some young teachers cannot avoid the heavy teaching and scientific research loads. Instead, they cleverly use the students in the classroom as a resource for their own academic inquiry, conduct research on students’ classroom behavior (strategic consideration), and regard students as a tool for their own research (moral consideration). However, they want to be an ideal university teacher when seeking their identity (who to be); that is, they advocate that people are the purpose in themselves, and that the interaction between teachers and students is not utilitarian. This shows the complexity of academic work as a reflective practice.

With the increasing complexity of academic work, the focus of academic workers is constantly changing. The ideal reflective practitioner seeks to achieve coherence between considerations: “not to separate methods and goals, but to interactively define each other in framing a problem” (Schön, 2018,p.57). When faced with difficulties in academic work, young teachers in universities and colleges can use the above-described framework of questions to continuously carry out reflection through actions, experience, and trial and error to finally achieve unity among strategic, evaluative, moral, and identity considerations with the ultimate goal of solving practical problems in academic work. Because academic work is not completely regulated by the academic management system, academic work itself can be the subject of reflection, negotiation, and striving.

Finally, Schon (2018) argues that “reflective practitioners should conduct “evaluation experiments” when selecting practical problems” (p.112). This should be a process of self-assessment rather than an assessment of others. For example, young teachers in universities and colleges can evaluate the results of their actions, including through the following questions: “Do I like or approve of the results?”, “Am I really a good teacher?”, and “Does this conflict with my beliefs and values?”. However, the evaluation experiment is not summative evaluation; rather, it spans every aspect of the problem-solving process, and it constantly changes with the situation. This is also a process of “reflective turning,” through which teachers can gain insights from their experiences, discover the power of change, and develop the knowledge and ability to change their way of life.

 

 

 

