# Conclusion

As the push for academic system reform becomes stronger at the grassroots level, the management of academic performance and output is becoming more intense. The “input-output” evaluation model has become the basis of the current academic management system. Within this environment, young teachers must construct a new system that integrates performance management and practical rationality into the academic organization to allow for the emergence of an effective academic community. This paper suggests that young teachers in universities and colleges should re-consider the nature of academic work and become reflective practitioners. To be a reflective practitioner means that academics are not bound by the dichotomy of practical rationality and do not rely solely on existing theories or technologies to act; rather, they consider the practice of academic work as “ideological action,” “thinking with action,” “reflecting in academic work,” “knowing in academic work,” and “actively reflecting on academic work.” These phrases emphasize the continuous and dynamic process of engaging with academic work in a thoughtful and reflective manner.

This article presents a reflective framework for academic work based on a series of strategic, evaluative , moral, and identity questions that guide young teachers to establish a reflective practice in their academic work. Given the continuous change in the academic field of work, the problem framework for reflective practitioners presented in this paper is also procedural and developmental. Based on the results of case studies, this framework provides a vision for expanding the boundaries of academic worker actions without dismantling the rigid academic system. In this approach, teachers in colleges and universities constantly engage in “the reflective turn” to restore the vitality of the academic system.



Becoming a reflective practitioner enriches the nature of academic work and allows the teacher to embrace their brilliance as a person, resulting in greater educational creativity.

